{"title":"The corporate tax rate really matters","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/957285557/0/marginalrevolution~The-corporate-tax-rate-really-matters.html","date":1779781878000,"content":"<blockquote><p>Three findings emerge. First, improvements in aggregate tax competitiveness are positively and significantly associated with real GDP per capita growth, robust to a wide range of controls. Second, this aggregate effect is driven entirely by the corporate tax pillar; no other component displays a significant growth effect. Third, the corporate tax effect materializes contemporaneously and accumulates over time, with a statistically significant three-year cumulative effect of approximately 0.16 percentage points per one-point improvement in the corporate tax score. These results suggest that the full architecture of the corporate tax system, not merely the headline statutory rate, is what matters for growth.</p></blockquote>\r\n<p>That is from <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.econstor.eu/bitstream/10419/340903/1/GLO-DP-1754.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">a recent paper</a> by Michael Christla and Monika Köppl–Turyna.  Via the excellent <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://x.com/samirvarma\" target=\"_blank\">Samir Varma</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/05/the-corporate-tax-rate-really-matters.html\">The corporate tax rate really matters</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/957285557/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/957285557/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/fblike20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.